\begin{document}

\title{Adaptive Temporal Graph Learning for Network Anomaly Detection Under Concept Drift}

\author{Dr. Arpita Dutta\inst{1} \and Sayak Sengupta\inst{2} \and Avinanda Guchait\inst{3}}

\institute{
Department of Computer Science Engineering (AIML), Techno Main Salt Lake, Kolkata, India\\
\email{john@example.com}
\and
ISG DevOps, Keysight Technologies, Kolkata, India\\
\email{sayak.sengupta@keysight.com}
\and
Department of Computer Science Engineering (Data Science), Techno Main Salt Lake, Kolkata, India\\
\email{avinandaguchait123@gmail.com}
}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Network intrusion detection is often framed as a static i.i.d.\ classification task, despite real traffic being relational and non-stationary. We study a temporal graph {\color{red}IDS} that represents traffic as per-window graphs, encodes them with a Graph Attention Network (GAT), tracks them with an LSTM, and adapts via an ADWIN drift detector, evaluated under a strictly time-ordered, never-shuffled protocol. {\color{red}On CIC-IDS2017 (2{,}572{,}640 flows, 2{,}577 windows, five days), full retraining after detected drift raises rolling F1 from 0.195 to 0.353 at $157\times$ the wall-clock cost and $3.9\times$ the peak GPU memory of incremental fine-tuning, which gives no measurable benefit (0.196 vs.\ 0.195) --- a negative result reported rather than omitted. Under the classical stationary split, every window-level model collapses to F1~$\approx$~0, traced to a train/test anomaly-rate shift induced by the split itself (13.4\% vs.\ 84.3\%) rather than a modeling failure, since the same architecture reaches F1 0.195 and ROC-AUC 0.61 across the full sequence. We frame the contribution as a drift-aware evaluation methodology for temporal graph IDS --- not a new architecture --- reporting both favorable and unfavorable outcomes.} {\color{red}A cross-dataset replication on NSL-KDD, UNSW-NB15 and TON\_IoT (per-flow scoring, 3 seeds) shows that every supervised model scores F1 $\ge$ 0.93 on a stationary hold-out yet loses up to 0.24 F1 under drift; that, unadapted, the GAT+LSTM does not beat flow-level baselines (mean stream F1 0.828 vs.\ 0.882 for Random Forest); and that drift-triggered adaptation lifts it to 0.884 (full retrain) or 0.877 (incremental, about $40$--$50\times$ cheaper), on par with an unadapted Random Forest that received no adaptation.}

\keywords{Network Intrusion Detection \and Graph Neural Networks \and Concept Drift \and Temporal Graph Learning \and CIC-IDS2017.}
\end{abstract}

% =============================================================================
\section{Introduction}
% =============================================================================

Real network traffic is dynamic: the mix of applications, users, and attacks observed today is not
the mix observed next week. Yet intrusion detection systems (IDS) are almost universally built and
evaluated under a stationary assumption --- trained once, tested on a random held-out split of the
\emph{same} corpus, and deployed as if that distribution persists indefinitely. This produces
impressive published accuracy but does not reflect deployment, a mismatch noted as early as Sommer
and Paxson's critique of ML evaluation practice in network security \cite{sommer2010closedworld}.

{\color{blue}The combination of users, apps, and threats seen today will not be the same as the combination seen tomorrow since real network traffic is dynamic. However, intrusion detection systems (IDS) are almost always conceived and evaluated under a stationary assumption: they are trained, tested on a random held-out split of the same corpus, and deployed as though that distribution is static. As early as Sommer and Paxson's evaluation of ML methodology in the context of network security \cite{sommer2010closedworld}, this provides excellent published correctness but fails to reflect deployment. 

A new design is motivated by two characteristics of network traffic. The first is that the traffic is relational: similar flows are eliminated by a row-by-row classifier but preserved by a graph representation, a good fit for graph neural networks (GNNs). The second is that a temporal encoder is needed on top of the per-slice embeddings, as the traffic also \emph{evolves temporally}, thus a per-slice graph encoder cannot capture the behavior changes \emph{across} slices. When viewed as a whole, these suggest that a graph-structured and temporal aware detector should degrade more gracefully under concept drift than a static flow-classifier, provided it can simultaneously detect and adapt to drift without retraining from scratch each time. CIC-IDS2017 provides a concrete example of this: Monday is completely benign, and every subsequent day features a new attack family (brute-force, denial-of-service, web attack/infiltration, botnet/port-scan/DDoS). A model which is trained only on the first two days and is never updated will, by definition, never encounter what comes next.}

Two properties of network traffic motivate a different design. Traffic is \emph{relational}: which
flows resemble one another is discarded by a row-by-row classifier but preserved by a graph
representation, a natural fit for graph neural networks (GNNs). Traffic also \emph{evolves
temporally}, so a per-slice graph encoder alone cannot capture how behavior changes \emph{across}
slices --- a temporal encoder on top of the per-slice embeddings is needed. Together, these suggest
a detector that is both graph-structured and temporally aware should degrade more gracefully under
\emph{concept drift} than a static flow classifier, provided it can also \emph{detect} drift and
\emph{adapt} without a full retrain every time. CIC-IDS2017 illustrates this directly: Monday is
entirely benign, and each subsequent day introduces a new attack family (brute-force,
denial-of-service, web attack/infiltration, botnet/port-scan/DDoS) --- a model trained only on the
first two days and never updated will, by construction, never have seen what comes after.

The objective of this work is to evaluate whether a simple GAT--LSTM temporal graph model can maintain anomaly-detection performance under such temporal distribution shifts when evaluated using a strictly time-ordered protocol. The study focuses on evaluation methodology and adaptation behavior rather than proposing a new graph or temporal architecture. The main contributions of  this work includes \textit{ drift aware evaluation methodology} where we evaluate network anomaly detection using a strictly time-ordered, never-shuffled protocol in which models are trained only on past observations and evaluated on subsequent traffic containing different attack phases,\textit{ graph-temporal detection pipeline} where we represent each traffic window as a flow-similarity graph, encode the graph using a Graph Attention Network (GAT), model temporal dependencies using an LSTM, and monitor the learned representation with ADWIN for drift detection,\textit{ accuracy and adaptation-cost analysis}  in which we compare no adaptation, incremental fine-tuning, and full retraining under the same drift-detection framework and report both detection performance and adaptation cost, including wall-clock time and peak GPU memory.  Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews related work; Section~\ref{sec:problem} formalizes the
problem; Section~\ref{sec:method} describes the pipeline; Section~\ref{sec:experiments} reports
every experiment; Section~\ref{sec:discussion} compares against prior work;
Section~\ref{sec:limitations} states limitations; Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.

% =============================================================================
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}
% =============================================================================

\subsection{ML-based intrusion detection}

The dominant paradigm for IDS research on public flow datasets is supervised, flow-level
classification: each flow is reduced to a fixed-length feature vector (CICFlowMeter produces
roughly 70 statistics per flow for CIC-IDS2017 \cite{sharafaldin2018cicids2017}), and a classifier
--- Random Forest, XGBoost \cite{chen2016xgboost}, MLPs, or autoencoders --- separates benign from
attack traffic. This body of work almost universally reports a random or otherwise i.i.d.\ split,
which by construction cannot expose degradation under a shifting distribution; Experiments A and B
(Section~\ref{sec:experiments}) reproduce this classical protocol and then depart from it to make
the gap concrete. Several contemporaneous studies benchmark this same paradigm on CIC-IDS2017 and
related tasks, directly comparable to the flow-level baselines in Table~\ref{tab:expa}: Vedash
et~al.\ \cite{vedash2026xai} benchmark Random Forest, XGBoost, and a 1D CNN on the identical
dataset; Akanksha \cite{akanksha2025malware} and Ravi and Poornima \cite{ravi2025flowmetadata}
report similarly near-perfect tree-ensemble results on related flow-classification tasks; and
R{\'i}os-Guiral et~al.\ \cite{riosguiral2024flowspackets} compare flow- and packet-level
representations, a granularity question adjacent to this paper's flow-vs-window distinction
(Section~\ref{sec:method}). Viksha and Singh \cite{viksha2024sampling} show that traffic
\emph{sampling} affects ML-IDS accuracy, paralleling this paper's Experiment A finding that a naive
chronological split degrades window-level detection (Section~\ref{sec:expa}). Unsupervised
alternatives such as $k$-means network slicing \cite{aouedi2021unsupervised} and
hierarchical-clustering-based flow reduction \cite{su2018hierarchical} offer a different route to
the imbalance/scale problem than this paper's fixed-size windowing (Section~\ref{sec:limitations});
tabular ML is also applied across network-measurement tasks beyond security, e.g.\ optical-network
traffic forecasting \cite{szostak2020lda}.

\subsection{Graph neural networks for cybersecurity}

A growing line of work represents network traffic as a graph and applies GNNs --- typically GCN
\cite{kipf2017gcn} or GAT \cite{velickovic2018gat}, surveyed in \cite{zhou2020gnnreview} --- to
classify nodes, edges, or whole graphs as malicious, using host-communication graphs or, when host
identity is unavailable, flow-similarity graphs (the design this paper is forced into,
Section~\ref{sec:method}). The common limitation, and the gap Experiments B/C target, is that the
graph is treated as \emph{static per snapshot} rather than as a tracked, adapted sequence.
The systematic review of Ares-Robledo et~al.\ \cite{aresrobledo2026gnnsurvey}, covering 79
dynamic/temporal GNN anomaly-detection studies in cybersecurity, reaches a consistent conclusion:
GCN, GAT, and hybrid spatiotemporal models dominate, yet many studies do not report enough detail on
graph construction or \emph{temporal splitting} to judge robustness, evaluation concentrates on a
small set of reused protocols, accuracy/ROC-style metrics can mislead under severe class skew, and
continual-learning or drift-adaptive implementations tailored to dynamic anomaly detection remain
rare, so practical deployments ``may still hinge on frequent retraining.'' These are precisely the
reporting gaps this paper's strictly time-ordered protocol, explicit graph-construction description,
and adaptation-cost measurements are designed to close.

\subsection{Temporal graphs and concept drift}

Dynamic-graph architectures such as Temporal Graph Networks (TGN) \cite{rossi2020tgn} and
EvolveGCN \cite{pareja2020evolvegcn} extend GNNs to graph sequences with more sophisticated memory
and parameter-evolution mechanisms than a recurrent layer over per-snapshot embeddings. This paper
deliberately uses the simpler GNN$\rightarrow$LSTM \cite{hochreiter1997lstm} design instead: given
a single-GPU, few-week execution budget, a more complex temporal-GNN architecture risked displacing
the drift-detection and adaptation-cost experiments that are this paper's core empirical claim.
Concept-drift detection is a mature sub-field of streaming ML, with detectors such as ADWIN
\cite{bifet2007adwin} and distributional-distance measures \cite{gama2014survey} largely
developed outside the network-security domain. This paper imports ADWIN, via the \emph{river}
streaming ML library \cite{montiel2021river}, as a drift detector over a scalar summary of the
model's own learned representation (the L2 norm of the LSTM output $Z_t$), rather than inventing a
new detector --- the contribution is the application, and the resulting adaptation-cost
measurement, in this graph-temporal IDS setting.

\subsection{Drift-adaptive and continual learning for intrusion detection}

A recent line of work targets concept drift in IDS directly, mostly on tabular flow features rather
than graphs. NetGuard \cite{gupta2026netguard} combines density-aware active sampling with deep
generative augmentation to adapt an NIDS under drift and class imbalance, evaluated on CIC-IDS
2017/2018 (as a cross-year drift pair), UGR'16, and ACI-IoT23; it reports roughly $2\times$ average
detection improvement and 8--15$\times$ higher F1 on rare attacks with only 1\% labeled data. Its
emphasis is label efficiency and rare-class coverage; this paper instead asks what a drift-triggered
\emph{model update} costs in compute. SSF \cite{zhang2025ssf} is a continual-learning method with a
strategic sample-selection step (prioritizing samples that cause the drifted pattern) and a
strategic forgetting step that discards outdated samples when significant drift is detected; it
reports 90.50\% and 90.27\% accuracy on NSL-KDD and UNSW-NB15. SSF decides \emph{what} to keep in a
memory buffer, whereas this paper's three strategies decide \emph{how much} of the model to update,
and neither SSF nor NetGuard uses a graph or temporal encoder.

Drift-aware adaptation has also been studied outside IDS, and the design space is closely related.
ADA-ADF \cite{li2025adaadf} is an unsupervised streaming time-series anomaly detector that couples a
hybrid statistical/performance-based drift detector (separating sudden from incremental drift) with a
replay-based update whose replay ratio is drift-specific, evaluated on four univariate datasets.
DHDA \cite{xiang2026dhda}, for online configuration-performance learning, uses ADWIN to detect local
drift and combines cheap incremental updates with periodic full retraining, reporting up to $2\times$
improvement across eight software systems at reasonable overhead. CGLM \cite{zhou2025cglm} applies a
graph attention module to multi-stream concept drift, performing lightweight subgraph adjustment when
no drift is detected and regenerating the correlation graph when drift occurs; it targets traffic and
weather forecasting rather than security. All three adopt the same incremental-versus-full-update
split that Experiment D tests, but report accuracy gains, not the wall-clock/memory cost of each
branch, and none evaluates a graph-temporal IDS on a multi-day attack corpus.

\subsection{Federated and reinforcement-learning IDS}

A parallel literature addresses privacy and adaptivity through federated learning (FL) and
reinforcement learning (RL). Belenguer et~al.\ \cite{belenguer2025flreview} review FL for IDS
(LSTM, GRU, and autoencoder-based systems, many evaluated on CIC-IDS2017) and identify statistical
heterogeneity and device constraints as open problems. FedT-DQN \cite{ma2025fedtdqn} combines a
Transformer-based federated aggregator with a double Q-network and reports over 99\% accuracy on four
benchmark datasets. FL-MalDrift \cite{patel2026fmaldrift} runs ADWIN, DDM, EDDM, or HDDM on each
client and gates server aggregation on EWMA-smoothed drift scores, reporting 94.7\% (Drebin), 96.8\%
(CICMalDroid 2020), and 92.4\% (chronological AndroZoo split) accuracy; it is the closest of these to
this paper's use of ADWIN, but for Android malware and not network traffic. Suresh and Jose
\cite{suresh2025ppo} use Proximal Policy Optimization to reweight Random Forest and CatBoost base
learners under an MLP meta-learner, reporting 97.16\% average accuracy over five datasets (including
CICIDS). These systems optimize accuracy, privacy, or ensemble weighting; none models traffic
relationally or time-orders the evaluation across attack phases.

\medskip
The gap this paper occupies is the intersection of the above: detection that is graph-structured
\emph{and} temporal \emph{and} drift-adaptive, evaluated together under one strictly time-ordered
protocol, with adaptation cost reported alongside accuracy, rather than each property demonstrated
in isolation as is typical of the literature above.

% =============================================================================
\section{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:problem}
% =============================================================================

Let a per-window communication graph be
\begin{equation}
G_t = (V_t, E_t),
\end{equation}
and let the full observed sequence be
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{G} = \{G_1, G_2, \ldots, G_T\}.
\end{equation}
The detector is a function of the trailing $k$-window history:
\begin{equation}
f(\mathcal{G}_{t-k:t}) \rightarrow A_t, \qquad A_t \in [0,1],
\label{eq:anomaly-score}
\end{equation}
the anomaly score for window $t$. A concept-drift detector $D$ monitors a scalar statistic of the
model's learned representation stream; when it fires, the model is updated,
\begin{equation}
M_{t+1} = \mathrm{Update}(M_t, D_t).
\label{eq:update}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Node/edge definition used in this paper.} The originally intended formulation --- $V_t$
as network hosts and $E_t$ as who-talked-to-whom communication edges --- requires source/destination
IP addresses, absent from the CIC-IDS2017 data release available for this work
(Section~\ref{sec:data}). The formulation is therefore redefined: $V_t$ is the set of flows captured
in window $t$, and $E_t$ is the set of $k$-nearest-neighbor edges in scaled feature space, computed
independently per window ($k=8$, symmetrized). The index $t$ enumerates fixed-size, 1{,}000-row
chunks within a single day-file, in row order; no wall-clock timestamp exists in this data release,
so chronological order is assumed to equal row order within each day-file
(Section~\ref{sec:limitations}).

% =============================================================================
\section{Proposed Method}
\label{sec:method}
% =============================================================================

The pipeline (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}) turns raw per-day flow records into a sequence of window
graphs, encodes each graph spatially, tracks the resulting embeddings temporally, scores anomalies,
monitors for drift, and adapts the model when drift is detected.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  node distance=7mm and 9mm,
  box/.style={draw, rounded corners, fill=blue!6, align=center, font=\small,
              text width=2.9cm, minimum height=0.9cm},
  arr/.style={-{Latex[length=2.2mm]}, thick}
]

\node[box] (raw) {Raw flow records\\(5 days, CIC-IDS2017)};
\node[box, below=of raw] (win) {Feature scaling \& fixed-size windowing};
\node[box, below=of win] (graph) {Per-window $k$-NN\\similarity graph $G_t$};
\node[box, below=of graph] (gat) {GAT spatial encoder\\$H_t$};
\node[box, below=of gat] (lstm) {LSTM temporal encoder\\$Z_t$};
\node[box, below=of lstm] (score) {Anomaly score $A_t$ \&\\attack-type head};

\node[box, fill=orange!8, right=of lstm] (drift) {ADWIN drift\\monitor on $\|Z_t\|_2$};
\node[box, fill=green!8, below=of drift] (adapt) {Adaptation:\\no-adapt / incremental /\\full retrain};

\draw[arr] (raw) -- (win);
\draw[arr] (win) -- (graph);
\draw[arr] (graph) -- (gat);
\draw[arr] (gat) -- (lstm);
\draw[arr] (lstm) -- (score);
\draw[arr] (lstm) -- (drift);
\draw[arr] (drift) -- node[left,font=\scriptsize]{fires} (adapt);
\draw[arr, dashed] (adapt) to[bend right=75] node[below,font=\scriptsize]{updates weights} (gat);

\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Pipeline overview. Raw flows are windowed and turned into per-window similarity graphs,
encoded spatially by a GAT and tracked temporally by an LSTM to produce an anomaly score. The same
temporal representation feeds an ADWIN drift monitor, which triggers one of three adaptation
strategies when it fires; a triggered strategy updates the spatial and/or temporal encoder before
the next window is scored.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Flow representation and graph construction}
Each flow is represented by roughly 70 CICFlowMeter numeric features (packet/byte counts,
inter-arrival-time statistics, TCP flag counts, subflow and bulk-transfer statistics, active/idle
timing, and destination port), standardized using statistics fit on Monday+Tuesday only so later
days' feature scales cannot leak into the stationary-split experiment; each scaled feature vector
becomes one node's feature vector $x$ in $G_t$. Within each fixed-size window of 1{,}000
consecutive flows (never spanning a day boundary), a $k$-nearest-neighbor graph is built over the
scaled feature vectors ($k=8$), then symmetrized into an undirected edge set. Node count varies per
window (all flows assigned to that window); no padding is used.

\subsection{Temporal modeling}
A 2-layer GAT ($4$ attention heads, hidden dimension $64$) encodes each window's graph,
\begin{equation}
H_t = \mathrm{GAT}(G_t),
\end{equation}
followed by mean-pool readout to a single per-window vector. An LSTM (hidden dimension $64$)
\cite{hochreiter1997lstm} is applied over the trailing $k=6$ window embeddings,
\begin{equation}
Z_t = \mathrm{LSTM}(H_{t-k:t}),
\end{equation}
producing the window's representation $Z_t$ used in Equation~\eqref{eq:anomaly-score}.

\subsection{Anomaly detection, training, and drift detection}
A sigmoid anomaly head produces $A_t = \sigma(f(Z_t)) \in [0,1]$; a secondary multi-class
attack-type head is trained jointly, with total loss $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{BCE}} +
0.5\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}$. Training uses Adam ($\mathrm{lr}=10^{-3}$) with mixed precision and
per-epoch checkpointing (model, optimizer, RNG state), verified resumable by a kill-and-resume
drill mid-epoch. ADWIN monitors the L2 norm of $Z_t$ across the full Monday--Friday sequence as a
cheap scalar summary of how the model currently represents traffic. Rather than injecting synthetic
drift, CIC-IDS2017's own day-file boundaries serve as native drift phases: Monday (benign
baseline), Tuesday (brute-force), Wednesday (DoS), Thursday (web attack/infiltration), Friday
(botnet/port-scan/DDoS).

\subsection{Adaptive model updating}
Three strategies are compared behind one interface:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \emph{No-adapt}: the model is never updated (control condition).
  \item \emph{Full retrain}: the model is discarded and retrained from scratch on every window
        observed so far, whenever ADWIN fires.
  \item \emph{Incremental}: only the LSTM and anomaly head are unfrozen and fine-tuned for 3
        epochs on a recent window of history (the GAT encoder is left untouched), whenever ADWIN
        fires.
\end{itemize}
Wall-clock time, peak GPU memory, and sample count are logged for every adaptation event.

\subsection{Explainability}
Last-layer GAT attention weights are extracted for true-positive anomaly windows and visualized as
the top-10 highest-attention edges, each node labeled by its destination port and truncated attack
label (the only identifying fields available), framed as ``which neighboring flows most influenced
this window's anomaly signal'' rather than a host-identity framing.

% =============================================================================
\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}
% =============================================================================

Every number and figure in this section is generated programmatically by the experiment pipeline
described in Section~\ref{sec:method}; none are hand-typed.

\subsection{Dataset}
\label{sec:data}

CIC-IDS2017 \cite{sharafaldin2018cicids2017} provides five days (eight capture files) of labeled
traffic. Table~\ref{tab:dataset} gives the flow count per file after cleaning (dropping NaN/Inf
rows and exact duplicates, and fixing known label-encoding typos in the community mirror). The
redistributed mirror used here (commonly named \emph{MachineLearningCVE}) contains only a
destination-port field, the $\sim$70 CICFlowMeter statistics, and a label field --- no
source/destination IP, source port, protocol, flow ID, or timestamp. This is the central data
constraint that reshaped the node/edge design in Section~\ref{sec:method} (flow-similarity graphs
rather than host-communication graphs) and is discussed further in Section~\ref{sec:limitations}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{CIC-IDS2017 flow counts after cleaning, and window-level composition after windowing
(1{,}000 flows/window). Window anomaly rate is the fraction of windows containing at least one
attack flow.}
\label{tab:dataset}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{|l|r|r|r|}
\hline
Day file & Flows & Windows & Anom.\ rate \\
\hline
Monday (benign baseline)        & 502{,}650 & 503 & 0.0\% \\
Tuesday (brute-force)            & 421{,}626 & 422 & 60.4\% \\
Wednesday (DoS)                  & 610{,}492 & 611 & 66.1\% \\
Thursday AM (web attacks)        & 164{,}179 & 165 & 39.4\% \\
Thursday PM (infiltration)       & 252{,}790 & 253 & 11.9\% \\
Friday AM (benign)               & 184{,}044 & 185 & 63.2\% \\
Friday PM (port scan)            & 213{,}777 & 214 & 54.7\% \\
Friday PM (DDoS)                 & 223{,}082 & 224 & 67.0\% \\
\hline
\textbf{Total} & \textbf{2{,}572{,}640} & \textbf{2{,}577} & \textbf{44.2\%} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

At the flow level the dataset is dominated by benign traffic ($\approx$83.5\% BENIGN overall) while at
the window level, because a window is labeled anomalous if \emph{any} flow within it is an attack,
the anomaly rate is considerably higher (44.2\% overall) and varies substantially by day, from 0\%
(Monday) to 67.0\% (Friday DDoS).

\subsection{Experimental protocol}
All splits are strictly \textbf{time-based} and no random shuffling is used anywhere in this paper.
Four experiments plus an ablation are run:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Experiment A --- Stationary performance.} Monday+Tuesday only, an 80/20
        chronological split (train on the first 80\% of Mon+Tue windows, test on the trailing
        20\%) --- the classical protocol used throughout the flow-level IDS literature.
  \item \textbf{Experiment B --- Temporal contribution.} Models trained once on Monday+Tuesday
        and \emph{never updated}, scored across the entire Monday-through-Friday sequence
        (2{,}577 windows), with rolling-window (width 20) metrics averaged over the run.
  \item \textbf{Experiment C --- Drift degradation.} Reuses Experiment B's run; plots rolling F1
        over the full sequence with day-boundary phase transitions marked.
  \item \textbf{Experiment D --- Adaptation comparison.} No-adaptation vs.\ full-retrain vs.\
        incremental, all drift-triggered by the same ADWIN detector across the same full
        sequence, with wall-clock time, peak GPU memory, and sample count logged per event.
  \item \textbf{Ablation.} Model1 (flow-only MLP) $\rightarrow$ Model2 (static GCN)
        $\rightarrow$ Model3 (GAT+LSTM, no adaptation) $\rightarrow$ Model4 (GAT+LSTM,
        incremental adaptation), isolating the graph, temporal, and adaptation contributions.
\end{itemize}

Metrics reported throughout --- F1, precision--recall AUC (PR-AUC), ROC-AUC, and false-positive
rate (FPR) --- are computed at a 0.5 decision threshold on the anomaly score $A_t$:
\begin{equation}
F1 = 2\cdot\frac{P\cdot R}{P+R}, \qquad
\mathrm{FPR} = \frac{FP}{FP+TN}.
\label{eq:metrics}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Hardware and software}
\label{sec:hw}
All experiments ran on a single laptop GPU (NVIDIA RTX 2000 Ada Generation, 8\,GB VRAM) under
Windows~11, with PyTorch 2.6.0, PyTorch Geometric 2.8.0.post1, the \emph{river} streaming library
0.23.0 (ADWIN), scikit-learn 1.7.2, XGBoost 3.2.0, pandas 2.3.3, and NumPy 2.2.6, using automatic
mixed precision throughout; peak GPU memory for the largest logged full-retrain event was
224.7\,MB, comfortably within an 8\,GB budget.

\subsection{Experiment A --- Stationary performance}
\label{sec:expa}

Table~\ref{tab:expa} reports every model's performance on the Monday+Tuesday, time-based 80/20
stationary split.

