Random Forest        & flow   & \textbf{0.9885} & 0.9991 & 1.0000 & 0.0000 \\
XGBoost               & flow   & 0.9862 & 0.9996 & 1.0000 & 0.00004 \\
MLP                   & flow   & 0.9512 & 0.9869 & 0.9997 & 0.00055 \\
Autoencoder            & flow   & 0.0000 & 0.0171 & 0.7661 & 0.00002 \\
Sequence LSTM          & window & 0.0000 & 0.7015 & 0.1837 & 0.0000 \\
Static GCN             & window & 0.0000 & 0.6707 & 0.0723 & 0.0000 \\
Static GAT             & window & 0.0000 & 0.6555 & 0.0000 & 0.0345 \\
\textbf{GAT+LSTM (proposed)} & window & \textbf{0.0000} & 0.6697 & 0.0641 & 0.0000 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\subsubsection{Flagged, not hidden.} Every flow-level tabular baseline is near-perfect, as is typical
of published CIC-IDS2017 results under a stationary split. Every window/graph-level model,
including the proposed model, collapses to $F1=0$, and several have ROC-AUC below 0.5 (worse than
random ranking). This is the failure mode the evaluation protocol is designed to flag rather than
silently report: an underperforming proposed model here signals a bug or evaluation artifact to
investigate, not a result to publish uncritically.

\subsubsection{Root cause.} Experiment A's window-level test set is small (185 windows) and drawn from
a chronological slice whose class balance differs sharply from training. Over Monday+Tuesday as a
whole, 27.6\% of the 925 windows are anomalous; but a chronological 80/20 split places the entire
185-window test set in Tuesday's brute-force period, where the anomaly rate is 84.3\%, against
13.4\% in the preceding 740-window training set (Table~\ref{tab:expa-imbalance}). The window-level
models are trained on a benign-dominated majority and evaluated on a short, heavily attack-skewed
tail unlike most of training. Table~\ref{tab:trainlog} corroborates this: training loss falls
monotonically from 0.465 to 0.018, while validation F1 never exceeds 0.061 and is 0 in 11 of 20
epochs --- a textbook overfitting/distribution-shift signature. This echoes Viksha and Singh's
\cite{viksha2024sampling} finding that IDS accuracy is sensitive to \emph{how} traffic is sampled
into a split, not only how much is used; here the chronological split itself concentrates an
entire attack phase into the test set.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Class balance driving Experiment A's degenerate window-level metrics.}
\label{tab:expa-imbalance}
\small
\begin{tabular}{|p{4.4cm}|r|r|}
\hline
Split (Mon+Tue windows) & $n$ & Anomaly rate \\
\hline
Full Mon+Tue           & 925 & 27.6\% \\
Train (first 80\%, chronological)  & 740 & 13.4\% \\
Test (trailing 20\%, chronological = all Tuesday) & 185 & 84.3\% \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{GAT+LSTM training log excerpt (full 20 epochs): training loss falls steadily while
held-out F1 stays near zero.}
\label{tab:trainlog}
\small
\begin{tabular}{|r|r|r|r|r|}
\hline
Epoch & Train loss & Val.\ F1 & Val.\ PR-AUC & Val.\ ROC-AUC \\
\hline
0  & 0.4654 & 0.000 & 0.6555 & 0.0000 \\
5  & 0.0647 & 0.000 & 0.6890 & 0.1519 \\
10 & 0.0337 & 0.036 & 0.6880 & 0.0853 \\
15 & 0.0322 & 0.035 & 0.6783 & 0.1001 \\
19 & 0.0183 & 0.000 & 0.6697 & 0.0641 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

This needs further investigation before Experiment A's numbers are treated as final; likely fixes
are a larger or rebalanced window size, a different split point, or per-class-weighted training. We
report it exactly as observed rather than adjusting the split after the fact for a better number.

\subsection{Experiment B --- Temporal contribution}
\label{sec:expb}

Unlike Experiment A's 185-window slice, Experiment B scores every model across the entire
2{,}577-window Monday-through-Friday sequence (rolling 20-window metrics, averaged over the run),
with each model trained once on Monday+Tuesday and never updated afterward.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Experiment B --- full Mon--Fri sequence, no adaptation, rolling-metric average.}
\label{tab:expb}
\small
\begin{tabular}{|l|r|r|r|r|}
\hline
Model & F1 & PR-AUC & ROC-AUC & FPR \\
\hline
Random Forest (flow, no adapt) & 0.1221 & 0.6999 & 0.7100 & 0.0000 \\
Static GCN (no adapt)          & 0.1202 & 0.6886 & 0.6384 & 0.0347 \\
\textbf{GAT+LSTM (no adapt)}   & \textbf{0.1946} & 0.6802 & 0.6083 & 0.1170 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

At full-sequence scale, the picture is markedly more sensible than Experiment A: all three models
rank better than random (ROC-AUC 0.61--0.71), and the proposed GAT+LSTM model has the best F1 of
the three, though at a higher false-positive rate than the flow-level Random Forest baseline ---
the first result consistent with the central hypothesis: the graph-temporal model outperforms both
a static flow classifier and a static graph classifier once evaluated across real day-to-day drift.

\subsection{Experiment C --- Drift degradation}
\label{sec:expc}

Figure~\ref{fig:drift} plots the GAT+LSTM's rolling F1 (window width 20) across the entire
Monday-through-Friday sequence, with vertical dashed lines marking the true day-file boundaries.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_drift_degradation.png}
\caption{Rolling F1 of the no-adaptation GAT+LSTM model across the full Mon--Fri sequence, with
day-boundary phase transitions marked.}
\label{fig:drift}
\end{figure}

The curve is highly non-stationary, as expected of a model never updated after Monday+Tuesday. Two
brief F1 spikes to $\approx 1.0$ occur early in Tuesday's brute-force period (window 600--800),
where the model was trained on adjacent data; a further spike appears near the
Tuesday$\to$Wednesday boundary before collapsing to $F1\approx0$ for almost all of Wednesday and
both Thursday segments --- the most direct illustration of the drift problem this paper studies,
since web attacks and infiltration are attack types never seen in training. Performance partially
recovers late in the sequence (F1 0.6--1.0 through the Friday port-scan and DDoS segments), likely
because those attack types produce flow-feature signatures closer to Monday+Tuesday training. This
uneven, day-dependent degradation is itself informative: concept drift here is a sequence of
distinct, differently-sized shocks, consistent with framing CIC-IDS2017's day boundaries as
discrete native drift events rather than continuous organic drift (Section~\ref{sec:limitations}).

\subsection{Experiment D --- Adaptation comparison}
\label{sec:expd}

Table~\ref{tab:expd-acc} reports accuracy under each adaptation strategy across the full sequence;
Table~\ref{tab:expd-cost} reports the cost of each adaptation event actually triggered by ADWIN.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Experiment D --- accuracy by adaptation strategy, full Mon--Fri sequence.}
\label{tab:expd-acc}
\small
\begin{tabular}{|l|r|r|r|r|}
\hline
Strategy & F1 & PR-AUC & ROC-AUC & FPR \\
\hline
No adaptation            & 0.1946 & 0.6802 & 0.6083 & 0.1170 \\
Incremental adaptation    & 0.1963 & 0.6773 & 0.6020 & 0.1240 \\
\textbf{Full retrain}     & \textbf{0.3531} & 0.6865 & 0.6446 & 0.2349 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Experiment D --- adaptation cost. ``Events'' are the number of times ADWIN triggered that
strategy across the full sequence; times/memory are averaged over those events.}
\label{tab:expd-cost}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{|l|r|r|r|r|}
\hline
Strategy & Events & Total time & Time/event & Peak GPU mem.\ \\
\hline
Full retrain  & 10 & 5\,h 46\,min & 34.57\,min & 224.1\,MB \\
Incremental   & 8  & 1.77\,min   & 13.25\,s   & 57.8\,MB \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsubsection{Reading.} Full retraining raises F1 by 81\% relative to doing nothing (0.353 vs.\
0.195), and modestly improves PR-AUC/ROC-AUC, but costs $\approx 157\times$ more wall-clock time
per event (34.57 min vs.\ 13.25 s) and $\approx 3.9\times$ more peak GPU memory (224.1 vs.\
57.8\,MB). The lightweight incremental strategy --- fine-tuning only the LSTM and anomaly head for
3 epochs on recent data --- essentially did not help (0.1963 vs.\ 0.1946 F1, within measurement
noise): cheap, last-layer-only adaptation, as implemented here, is not sufficient to recover full
retraining's accuracy gain. This is a real accuracy/cost trade-off rather than a ``best of both
worlds,'' and it is the paper's clearest, most decisively supported empirical claim. Both
strategies' peak GPU memory is small in absolute terms; the practically important cost
differentiator is wall-clock time, not memory.

