Model1/Model2 come from Experiment A's small, imbalanced test slice, while Model3/Model4 come from
the much larger full-sequence evaluation --- they are \emph{not} on a like-for-like scale and
should not be read as a single clean progression until Experiment A's split issue is resolved. At
face value the flow-only MLP appears to dominate, but this reflects its far larger test set
(184{,}626 flows vs.\ 185 windows) more than a genuine graph-vs-no-graph comparison; Experiment B
(Table~\ref{tab:expb}), evaluated at matched full-sequence scale, is the fairer comparison and
shows GAT+LSTM outperforming the flow-level Random Forest baseline (F1 0.195 vs.\ 0.122).

\subsection{Explainability}
\label{sec:explain}

Three attention figures were generated for correctly-flagged (true-positive) anomaly windows, using
the last GAT layer's attention weights, cross-checked against each window's ground-truth attack
label.

\begin{figure*}[!t]
\centering
\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.32\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_attention_0_w3853.png}
\caption{Window 3853 (Friday PM, port scan)}
\end{subfigure}
\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.32\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_attention_1_w4326.png}
\caption{Window 4326 (Friday PM, DDoS)}
\end{subfigure}
\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.32\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_attention_2_w4341.png}
\caption{Window 4341 (Friday PM, DDoS)}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{Top-10 last-layer GAT attention edges for three true-positive anomaly windows. Node
labels show destination port and truncated attack label.}
\label{fig:attention}
\end{figure*}

The three windows illustrate two distinct attention patterns, reported honestly rather than
presenting only the more flattering one. In window 4326 (Figure~\ref{fig:attention}b), the
attention graph is genuinely informative: a chain of four distinct benign-labeled flows connected
by high-weight edges, alongside a separate small self-attention cluster on a DDoS-labeled, port-80
node --- spread across multiple neighboring flows in a way an analyst could use to trace what
contributed to the anomaly score. In windows 3853 and 4341 (Figure~\ref{fig:attention}a, c), by
contrast, attention is almost entirely \emph{self}-concentrated: the top-10 edges are dominated by
one or two nodes attending overwhelmingly to themselves. This \emph{attention collapse} means that,
for two of three sampled windows, last-layer GAT attention is a considerably weaker explainability
signal than it first appears --- an honest limitation of the attention-only approach
(Section~\ref{sec:limitations}), not a result to gloss over.

\subsection{Cross-dataset evaluation: NSL-KDD, UNSW-NB15 and TON-IoT}
\label{sec:multi}

The CIC-IDS2017 experiments above rest on a single dataset with a handful of day-level drift
events. To test whether the findings generalize, the same ideas --- flow-similarity window graphs,
a GAT encoder, an LSTM over trailing windows, and ADWIN-triggered adaptation --- were re-run on
three further public corpora: NSL-KDD \cite{tavallaee2009nslkdd}, UNSW-NB15
\cite{moustafa2015unsw} and the network portion of TON\_IoT \cite{alsaedi2020toniot}, against the
same family of flow-level and graph-level baselines. This is a self-contained second protocol, not
a re-use of the CIC-IDS2017 numbers; its scores are \emph{not} comparable with
Tables~\ref{tab:expa}--\ref{tab:expd-cost}.

\subsubsection{Datasets and drift phases.} None of the three corpora carries a usable timestamp,
and the TON\_IoT file is a concatenation of per-attack-type blocks, so each is cast as an ordered
list of \emph{phases}: phase~0 is the training distribution and later phases form a chronological
test stream (Table~\ref{tab:multi-data}). Rows are shuffled within a phase with a fixed seed and cut
into windows of 256 flows that never span a phase boundary; preprocessing (signed-$\log$ of
heavy-tailed columns, standardization, one-hot encoding of categoricals) is fit on phase~0 only.
The nature of the drift differs by dataset and matters for interpretation: NSL-KDD's is
\emph{native} (KDDTest+ contains 17 attack types absent from KDDTrain+), UNSW-NB15's is
\emph{native but mild} (the official train/test sets are partitions of the same capture), and
TON\_IoT's is \emph{constructed} (an ``emerging attack family'' scenario: injection/XSS appear in
phase~2 and ransomware/MITM in phase~3), not a real capture timeline.

\begin{table*}[!t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-dataset protocol. Windows have 256 flows; attack rate is the fraction of attack
flows. ``Native'' drift comes from the official partition; ``constructed'' drift is the
attack-family scenario described in the text.}
\label{tab:multi-data}
\small
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|r|r|l|}
\hline
Dataset & Phase & Flows & Attack rate & Content \\
\hline
NSL-KDD   & 0 train  & 125{,}952 & 46.5\% & KDDTrain+ (23 labels) \\
          & 1 stream &  22{,}528 & 56.9\% & KDDTest+ (+17 unseen attack types) \\
\hline
UNSW-NB15 & 0 train  & 175{,}104 & 68.1\% & official training set \\
          & 1 stream &  82{,}176 & 55.1\% & official testing set \\
\hline
TON\_IoT  & 0 train  &  94{,}976 & 73.7\% & normal + backdoor, DDoS, DoS, scanning, password \\
          & 1 stream &  39{,}936 & 75.0\% & same families, held-out flows \\
          & 2 stream &  47{,}360 & 84.2\% & + injection, XSS (new) \\
          & 3 stream &  28{,}416 & 73.7\% & + ransomware, MITM (new) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\subsubsection{Formulation and protocol.} Because these datasets are 47--76\% attacks, the
CIC-IDS2017 ``any attack in the window'' label would be constant, so every model here scores
\emph{each flow}: nodes are flows, edges are the same symmetrized $k=8$ nearest-neighbour graph
within a window, and the GAT+LSTM concatenates each node's GAT embedding with an LSTM state
computed over the mean-pooled embeddings of the trailing $K=6$ windows (including the target)
before a node-level classification head. Four graph-window variants are compared --- an MLP on
single flows (no graph, no time), a static GCN, a static GAT, and the GAT+LSTM --- alongside
flow-level Random Forest, XGBoost, a scikit-learn MLP, and a benign-only autoencoder (threshold at
the 95th percentile of training-benign reconstruction error). Phase~0 windows are split 80/10/10
into train, validation (early stopping), and a \emph{stationary hold-out}; every model is then scored
over the full stream with no update, and the GAT+LSTM is additionally run under drift-triggered
\emph{incremental} (LSTM and head, 3 epochs, last 50 windows) and \emph{full-retrain} (from scratch
on all windows seen, 10 epochs) adaptation. As in Experiment D, adaptation uses the labels of windows
\emph{already scored}, and each window is always scored before the model is adapted on it. All
numbers are means over 3 seeds; metrics are pooled over flows at a 0.5 threshold (the autoencoder
uses its own threshold for F1/FPR). Flow-level baselines receive \emph{no} adaptation, so the
adaptive rows are not a like-for-like comparison with them (Section~\ref{sec:limitations}).

Two details of the drift detector differ from Experiment D and are disclosed because they were set
after inspecting the NSL-KDD stream's label-free statistics (not its labels or accuracy). First, two
ADWIN instances monitor the window embedding norm and the mean predictive entropy of the window; the
model adapts when either fires, subject to a 10-window cooldown. Second, because ADWIN's Hoeffding
bound presumes values on the order of $[0,1]$ while both statistics vary by only $10^{-2}$--$10^{-1}$
(so ADWIN with raw values never fired on NSL-KDD at $\delta\in\{0.002, 0.01, 0.05\}$), each statistic
is z-scored on the last 100 training windows and mapped into $[0,1]$; ADWIN is warm-started on those
windows.

\subsubsection{Results: stationary versus drifted performance.} Table~\ref{tab:multi-f1} gives F1
on the stationary hold-out and over the whole stream; Table~\ref{tab:multi-aucfpr} gives ROC-AUC and
FPR over the stream.

