Four observations follow. \textbf{(i) The stationary hold-out hides the problem.} Every supervised
model scores F1 0.93--0.999 on the stationary hold-out (UNSW-NB15 is the hardest, 0.93--0.96), yet
loses substantially once the stream begins --- Random Forest falls from 0.999 to 0.757 on NSL-KDD
and the unadapted GAT+LSTM from 0.988 to 0.786, a drop of 0.20--0.24 --- while the autoencoder, weak
in-distribution (0.60--0.92), degrades least on NSL-KDD (0.921 to 0.866) and is the best model
there. This reproduces on three independent corpora the qualitative point of Experiments A--B: a
random-partition score overstates deployed performance.
\textbf{(ii) Without adaptation, graph structure and temporal context do not beat flow-level
models.} The unadapted GAT+LSTM (mean stream F1 0.828) trails Random Forest (0.882), the
scikit-learn MLP (0.877) and XGBoost (0.868), and is within 0.01 of the static GAT
(0.837; per-dataset gaps are 0.003, 0.000 and $-$0.029, the last favouring the \emph{static} GAT)
but below the graph-free torch MLP (0.867). On these datasets, then, the LSTM contributes nothing
measurable on its own and the kNN-graph encoder alone does not help over a flow-level MLP; this
does \emph{not} reproduce the Experiment~B ordering in which the temporal model led. A plausible
reason is that windows are shuffled within a phase, so the only temporal signal available is
phase-level context, but we did not test this.
\textbf{(iii) Adaptation is where the graph-temporal model recovers.} Drift-triggered full
retraining raises the GAT+LSTM's stream F1 by 0.055 on NSL-KDD, 0.013 on UNSW-NB15 and 0.101 on
TON\_IoT, lifting its three-dataset mean from 0.828 to 0.884 --- level with the best static model
(Random Forest, 0.882) and ahead of it on NSL-KDD (0.841 vs.\ 0.757) but behind it on
UNSW-NB15 (0.861 vs.\ 0.917) and TON\_IoT (0.951 vs.\ 0.971). Unlike in CIC-IDS2017,
\emph{incremental} adaptation also helps: it captures most of the full-retrain gain
(+0.033, +0.018, +0.097) and has the same mean as the scikit-learn MLP (0.877). Because the
flow-level baselines were never retrained, this is evidence that adaptation matters, not that the
graph-temporal model is the better architecture; a drift-triggered Random Forest or XGBoost would
be the fair comparator and was not run. \textbf{(iv) Adaptation reshapes the error profile.} On
UNSW-NB15 the unadapted graph models over-flag benign traffic on the stream (FPR 0.40, against
0.138 for Random Forest); adaptation cuts this to 0.16--0.18, which accounts for most of that
dataset's small F1 gain, while on NSL-KDD and TON\_IoT incremental adaptation buys recall at the
price of a higher FPR (0.121 and 0.113 vs.\ 0.080 and 0.069 unadapted), visible as lower ROC-AUC on
NSL-KDD (0.889 vs.\ 0.925).

\subsubsection{Per-phase behaviour on TON\_IoT.} TON\_IoT is the only dataset with several stream
phases, which separates the cases where drift is real from where it is not
(Table~\ref{tab:multi-ton}, Figure~\ref{fig:multi-stream}c). On phase~1, which re-draws the
training families, every supervised model matches its stationary score (F1 0.977--0.999) and
\emph{no} adaptation event fires. When a new attack family appears the picture splits: Random Forest
holds up (0.997 on injection/XSS, 0.866 on ransomware/MITM) while XGBoost falls to 0.581 in phase~3
despite a 0.990 ROC-AUC --- its scores still rank flows well but the fixed 0.5 threshold is wrong
for the new traffic, a drift failure that ROC-AUC alone would not reveal. The static GCN collapses
in phase~3 (0.285, with a seed standard deviation of 0.244), and the unadapted GAT+LSTM falls to
0.669 versus 0.758 for the static GAT. Both adaptive variants recover (0.885 incremental, 0.882
full retrain), exceeding every unadapted flow-level model in phase~3, Random Forest (0.866)
included, though they remain below Random Forest in phase~2 (0.951/0.961 vs.\ 0.997). Detection is delayed rather than immediate: events occur 31 and 38 windows after the
phase-2 and phase-3 boundaries, and the unadapted curves in Figure~\ref{fig:multi-stream}c show
the cost of that lag.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{TON\_IoT F1 per stream phase, mean of 3 seeds (phase~1: training families; phase~2:
+injection, XSS; phase~3: +ransomware, MITM).}
\label{tab:multi-ton}
\small
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|r|r|r|}
\hline
Model & Adapt. & Phase 1 & Phase 2 & Phase 3 \\
\hline
Random Forest      & none & 0.999 & 0.997 & 0.866 \\
XGBoost            & none & 0.999 & 0.991 & 0.581 \\
