\subsubsection{Adaptation cost.} Table~\ref{tab:multi-cost} reports the cost of the adaptation events
that ADWIN triggered. Full retraining costs about 40--50$\times$ the wall-clock time of incremental
fine-tuning (20--28\,s vs.\ 0.5--0.7\,s per event), a smaller ratio than the $157\times$ of
CIC-IDS2017 because these training sets are far smaller (450--680 windows of 256 flows against
$\approx$2{,}500 windows of 1{,}000). Peak GPU memory is essentially identical for the two strategies
(322--339\,MB) because this figure is a process-wide peak dominated by the GPU-resident window store,
so unlike Table~\ref{tab:expd-cost} it does not isolate the adaptation's own footprint and should
not be read as ``no memory difference''. Event counts are low (about 1--2 per seed per strategy) and
differ between strategies because adaptation changes later statistics. On NSL-KDD and UNSW-NB15 the
first event fires at stream window 59 in every run, i.e.\ detection takes roughly 60 windows even
though NSL-KDD's shift is present from the first window; the cost of that delay is not isolated here.

\begin{table*}[!t]
\centering
\caption{Adaptation cost on the new datasets (mean over events and 3 seeds; ``events'' = events per
seed). Peak memory is process-wide (see text).}
\label{tab:multi-cost}
\small
\begin{tabular}{|l|r|r|r|r|r|r|}
\hline
 & \multicolumn{2}{|c|}{Events} & \multicolumn{2}{|c|}{Time/event (s)} & \multicolumn{2}{|c|}{Peak mem (MB)} \\
\hline
Dataset & Incr. & Full & Incr. & Full & Incr. & Full \\
\hline
NSL-KDD   & 1.0 & 1.0 & 0.51 & 20.2 & 321.9 & 331.1 \\
UNSW-NB15 & 1.7 & 1.7 & 0.68 & 28.5 & 338.4 & 338.8 \\
TON\_IoT  & 1.7 & 2.3 & 0.54 & 27.4 & 336.5 & 333.7 \\
