\end{table*}

\subsubsection{Cross-dataset synthesis.} Three conclusions are consistent across the four corpora.
First, in-distribution scores are saturated and uninformative: all supervised models reach
F1 $\ge$ 0.93 on the stationary hold-out, so the ranking of methods is decided only by what happens
under drift. Second, drift hurts in proportion to how novel the new traffic is --- largest for
NSL-KDD's unseen attacks and TON\_IoT phase~3, and smallest for UNSW-NB15, whose train/test split is
statistically similar (every supervised model's stream F1 is within 0.04--0.10 of its stationary
F1, against drops of up to 0.24 on NSL-KDD). Third, the value of the graph-temporal design lies in how readily it can be
\emph{updated}, not in its static accuracy: the unadapted GAT+LSTM never beats a tuned Random
Forest, and its adapted version only matches it on average. Where the incremental strategy was a
negative result on CIC-IDS2017, it is a useful cheap option here; the difference may reflect the
dataset or our choices (a $10\times$ larger fine-tuning rate, a two-statistic detector, and
node-level rather than window-level labels all changed at once), and we did not isolate which.

% =============================================================================
\section{Comparison to Other Work}
\label{sec:discussion}
% =============================================================================

Table~\ref{tab:comparison} summarizes how this paper's protocol and headline result relate to the
representative studies reviewed in Section~\ref{sec:related}.

\begin{table*}[!t]
\centering
\caption{This work compared with representative prior CIC-IDS2017 / GNN-IDS studies.}
\label{tab:comparison}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
\newcolumntype{Y}{>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{|>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{2.9cm}|Y|Y|Y|Y|}
\hline
\textbf{Study} & \textbf{Representation} & \textbf{Split protocol} & \textbf{Drift-adaptive} & \textbf{Headline result} \\
\hline\hline
Vedash et~al.\ \cite{vedash2026xai} & Flow (tabular) & Random / stratified & No & F1 $\approx$0.98 (tree ensemble) \\
\hline
Akanksha \cite{akanksha2025malware} & Flow (tabular) & Random & No & Accuracy $\approx$0.97--0.99 \\
\hline
