\hline
Zhou and Lu (CGLM) \cite{zhou2025cglm} & Learned correlation graph over streams (non-IDS) & Small-data training, online test & Yes (subgraph / graph regeneration) & Beats baselines (forecasting) \\
\hline
\textbf{This work} & Flow-similarity graph sequence & \textbf{Strict time order, cross-day} & \textbf{Yes} (ADWIN + retrain/incremental) & F1 0.353 (adapted) vs.\ 0.195 (static) under drift \\
\hline
\end{tabularx}
\end{table*}

\subsubsection{Flow-level baselines match the published literature.} The near-perfect Random
Forest/XGBoost/MLP numbers in Table~\ref{tab:expa} (F1 0.95--0.99, ROC-AUC $\approx$1.0) are
consistent with the broad body of published CIC-IDS2017 results
\cite{sharafaldin2018cicids2017,vedash2026xai,akanksha2025malware,ravi2025flowmetadata}. This
paper reproduces that result under a \emph{chronological} rather than random split, and the numbers
hold up almost unchanged --- a useful negative control: with this much redundant per-flow signal,
switching to a time-based split barely matters at the flow level. This paper additionally asks what
happens to that same signal once evaluation moves to window/graph level across genuine day-to-day
drift (Experiments B--D) --- exactly where the near-perfect picture stops holding.

\subsubsection{Where this evaluation departs from prior work.} Most published GNN-IDS work reports a
single number on a static, randomly- or stratified-split graph dataset and does not evaluate across
a period long enough to contain a genuine, unseen distribution shift. Experiments B/C are, to our
knowledge, a comparatively rare instance of scoring a graph-temporal IDS model across an entire
multi-day corpus with no retraining in between, and the static-versus-temporal distinction matters
here (Table~\ref{tab:expb}): the temporal model's F1 advantage over a static GCN (0.195 vs.\ 0.120)
only appears at full-sequence scale and would be invisible under single-snapshot evaluation.
Concept-drift detectors are well studied on generic streaming benchmarks
\cite{bifet2007adwin,gama2014survey}, but published network-IDS papers rarely report the
\emph{wall-clock and memory cost} of adaptation alongside its accuracy gain; Experiment D's joint
accuracy/cost tables (Tables~\ref{tab:expd-acc}--\ref{tab:expd-cost}) fill that gap, and the finding
that cheap incremental fine-tuning does not recover full retraining's gain is a useful counterpoint
to the assumption that partial updates are ``nearly as good, for a fraction of the cost.''

\subsubsection{Against drift-adaptive IDS and continual-learning work.} NetGuard
\cite{gupta2026netguard} is the closest IDS comparator: it also uses CIC-IDS2017 and also finds that
a static model degrades under drift (it shows the same attack classes occupying different regions of
feature space between CIC-IDS 2017 and 2018). The two works are complementary rather than competing.
NetGuard reduces \emph{labeling} cost and strengthens rare-class (web attack, botnet) coverage on
flow features; this paper holds labels fixed and measures \emph{compute} cost of adaptation on a
graph-temporal model. The finding here that Thursday's web-attack and infiltration phases collapse to
F1~$\approx$~0 for a never-updated model is consistent with NetGuard's emphasis on rare, emerging
attack classes, and suggests that label-efficient sample selection could be paired with the
retraining trigger studied here. SSF \cite{zhang2025ssf} tackles a similar question from the
memory side (which samples to retain or forget); the full-retrain strategy here retains \emph{all}
history, and SSF's forgetting mechanism is a natural candidate for reducing the 34.6\,min per-event
retrain cost. We did not run either method, so no numerical superiority is claimed; reported
accuracies in Table~\ref{tab:comparison} are on different datasets, tasks, and protocols and are not
directly comparable to our F1.

\subsubsection{Against the incremental-versus-full-update literature.} The strategy split in
Experiment D mirrors choices made in DHDA \cite{xiang2026dhda} (ADWIN-triggered local drift
detection with incremental updates and periodic full retraining), CGLM \cite{zhou2025cglm}
(lightweight subgraph adjustment without drift, graph regeneration with drift), and ADA-ADF
\cite{li2025adaadf} (drift-type-specific replay). Those works report that a hybrid of cheap and
expensive updates pays off; our result qualifies this for a graph-temporal IDS. When the cheap path
only fine-tunes the LSTM and head (GAT frozen), it gave no measurable gain (F1 0.196 vs.\ 0.195), so
the benefit of a hybrid scheme appears to depend on \emph{what} the cheap path updates and on how
the drift signal is chosen, not merely on having one. DHDA's design points to a refinement we have
not tried: retraining only the affected region/sub-model rather than the whole network. Unlike
these studies, we also report absolute wall-clock and GPU-memory cost per event (Table~\ref{tab:expd-cost}).
Finally, ADWIN over a scalar summary of the model's own representation ($\|Z_t\|_2$) is
a lighter detector than the hybrid statistical/performance detector of ADA-ADF, and FL-MalDrift
\cite{patel2026fmaldrift} notes ADWIN's low false-alarm behavior, but we did not compare
detectors, which we leave to future work.

\subsubsection{Against federated, RL, and ensemble IDS.} FedT-DQN \cite{ma2025fedtdqn}, the PPO
ensemble of Suresh and Jose \cite{suresh2025ppo}, and the FL systems surveyed by Belenguer
et~al.\ \cite{belenguer2025flreview} report accuracies in the 97--99\% range, in line with the
flow-level baselines of Table~\ref{tab:expa}. Such numbers come from per-dataset evaluations where
train and test share a distribution, which is exactly the regime where our own flow-level models
score F1 0.95--0.99 yet our window-level models do not transfer across days. We therefore read the
high accuracies as evidence of the stationary-evaluation effect this paper highlights, not as
contradicting its findings. Privacy-preserving training (FL) and adaptive ensembling (RL) are
orthogonal to our contribution, and a federated deployment of the graph-temporal model would face
the client-side drift handling that FL-MalDrift addresses.

\subsubsection{Honest positioning.} This paper does not claim to outperform the flow-level literature's
headline numbers (Table~\ref{tab:expa}'s flow-level baselines beat every window-level model at that
scale), nor a novel architecture (GAT, LSTM, and ADWIN are all prior work). Its contribution is
narrower: showing concretely, on one real dataset, where a temporal graph model's advantage over
static baselines does and does not appear depending on the evaluation protocol, and quantifying
what recovering that advantage under drift actually costs.

% =============================================================================
\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}
% =============================================================================

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
\item This study has several limitations. The evaluation uses only CIC-IDS2017, whose available copy lacks host identifiers and timestamps; consequently, flow-similarity graphs are used and row order is treated as a proxy for chronology. The observed drift consists of a small number of day-level transitions, and the stationary window-level experiment is affected by a split-induced class-distribution shift that requires further validation. In addition, the graph and temporal hyperparameters were not exhaustively tuned, and the attention-based explanations were evaluated on only a small number of anomaly windows. The cross-dataset study (Section~\ref{sec:multi}) adds further caveats: NSL-KDD, UNSW-NB15 and TON\_IoT have no usable timestamps, so rows are shuffled within a phase and the LSTM can exploit only phase-level context; TON\_IoT's drift is a constructed attack-family scenario, and UNSW-NB15's is mild; the flow-level baselines were never adapted, so the adaptive GAT+LSTM is not compared against an equally adaptive Random Forest or XGBoost; the drift detector's statistics and scaling were chosen after inspecting NSL-KDD's label-free statistics, detection lagged the true shift by 30--60 windows, and the stream protocol changed several things relative to Experiment D at once (node-level labels, a two-statistic detector, a larger incremental learning rate), so the reversal of the incremental-adaptation result is not attributed to a single cause; and results are from 3 seeds on a single data split.
\end{itemize}

% =============================================================================
\section{Conclusion and Future Scope}
\label{sec:conclusion}
% =============================================================================

This study evaluated network anomaly detection under temporal distribution shift using a flow-similarity graph representation, GAT--LSTM temporal modeling, and ADWIN-based adaptation on CIC-IDS2017. Under the full time-ordered Monday--Friday evaluation, the GAT--LSTM model achieved an F1 of 0.195 without adaptation, while ADWIN-triggered full retraining increased F1 to 0.353. In contrast, the incremental strategy used in this study changed F1 only marginally to 0.196. The results therefore show that temporal evaluation can reveal behavior that is not visible under conventional stationary evaluation and that adaptation effectiveness depends strongly on the update strategy and its computational cost. The stationary window-level experiment remains inconclusive because of the class-distribution shift introduced by its chronological split.

A cross-dataset replication on NSL-KDD, UNSW-NB15 and TON\_IoT (Section~\ref{sec:multi}) supports the central methodological point and qualifies the rest. Stationary scores are saturated (F1 $\ge$ 0.93) and misleading, with drops of up to 0.24 F1 under drift; the unadapted GAT+LSTM does not outperform flow-level baselines (mean stream F1 0.828 vs.\ 0.882 for Random Forest) and the LSTM adds nothing measurable over a static GAT; drift-triggered adaptation is what closes the gap (0.884 with full retraining, 0.877 incrementally at roughly $1/40$--$1/50$ of the wall-clock cost), but only to parity with an unadapted Random Forest, so the evidence favours adaptation, not the graph-temporal architecture itself. In contrast to CIC-IDS2017, incremental adaptation helped on all three datasets.

Future work will focus on three directions. First, evaluation should be extended to additional intrusion-detection datasets, including CSE-CIC-IDS2018, and the flow-level baselines should be given the same drift-triggered retraining so that the adaptive comparison is like-for-like. Second, datasets retaining host and flow identifiers should be used to construct host-communication graphs and capture lateral-movement information. Third, the stationary evaluation should be redesigned with a more representative temporal split, while additional adaptation strategies and hyperparameter settings should be investigated to improve the accuracy--cost trade-off.

 

